  "contributors": "Pang supplied the PAL retained-frame, RPL and BLA boundary questions. Hearthline derived sparse positive-area interpolation, the late funded intervals and finite census for his research program.",
  "formal_scope": "Positive prime-power area at k^4 with two natural-number funding inequalities implies stock RiemannHypothesis. This applies to ideal capacity or two-cell capacity at the knots. The general prime-area envelope and the price of one funded size are separately proved.",
  "paper_scope": "Eventual one-sided fit at fourth powers is equivalent to RH. All integer boards are funded in arbitrarily late intervals of radius sqrt(N) log(N); the printed finite control covers all sizes through one billion. No universal construction on all fourth powers is supplied.",
  "self_review": {
    "type": "author self-review",
    "convexity": "The upper estimate uses the chord across two positive-area readings; the lower estimate uses the secant through the preceding two readings. Quadratic errors attain order gap squared for arbitrary convex inputs.",
    "oscillation": "At 1/2 the prime-square subtraction supplies residue 4/3. The actual critical zero has residue 2/(rho*(rho+1)); other zeros may occur off the line. One-sided positivity rules out both eventual envelopes inside the stated amplitude.",
    "integer_windows": "The integer factorial correction is o(sqrt(n)); relative steps of normalized margin are O(1/(sqrt(n)*log(n)^2)). This proves full intervals around an unbounded sequence of crossings without specifying a gap bound.",
    "finite_control": "Every intermediate integer is covered by the proven inequalities; endpoint prime bins use outward bounds on log(p) and p*log(p). A second sieve counts primes independently."
  },
